      "content": "# Task\nThe Python script `buggy.py` (your current directory) fails when run with **Qiskit 2.5.0**.\nIt comes from a user question titled: \"How to compose a larger circuit onto a smaller circuit in Qiskit, adding extra quantum registers to some fixed list\"\n\nObserved error when running it on Qiskit 2.5.0:\n```\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"C:\\personal_webpage\\my-portfolio\\APR_code_gen\\Part2_Create_test\\reconstructed_cases\\apr_p30\\runs\\gpt6solp30\\tasks\\issue_453\\workspace\\buggy.py\", line 20, in <module>\r\n    circ.compose(qc1, [0, 1, 2, 3], inplace=True)\r\n  File \"C:\\personal_webpage\\my-portfolio\\APR_code_gen\\Part1_Create_code\\envs\\qiskit_2_5_0\\Lib\\site-packages\\qiskit\\circuit\\quantumcircuit.py\", line 2285, in compose\r\n    raise CircuitError(\r\nqiskit.circuit.exceptions.CircuitError: 'Cannot compose onto a circuit with fewer qubits (6 > 4).'\r\n\n```\n\nRepair `buggy.py` **in place** so that it runs successfully on Qiskit 2.5.0\nand still does what the original author intended (same circuit / computation).\n\n## Rules\n- Run the script with `$PY buggy.py` (Qiskit 2.5.0 is installed only in that interpreter).\n  Do not pip install, downgrade or upgrade packages. There is no internet access from the shell.\n- Fix the root cause. Do NOT hide the error with try/except, do NOT delete the failing logic,\n  and do NOT replace the program with something unrelated.\n- Keep the change minimal. Plots may be produced, but the script must not block or need a display.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Read `buggy.py` and reproduce the error with `$PY buggy.py`.\n2. Work out why it fails on Qiskit 2.5.0.\n3. Edit `buggy.py` (e.g. with `cat <<'EOF' > buggy.py` or `sed -i`).\n4. Re-run `$PY buggy.py` until it exits with status 0.\n5. When done, submit by running exactly `echo COMPLETE_TASK_AND_SUBMIT_FINAL_OUTPUT` as a separate command.\n   You cannot continue after submitting.\n\nEach command runs in a fresh subshell; `cd` and environment changes do not persist.\n\n\n## Original user question (solution sections removed)\nTitle: How to compose a larger circuit onto a smaller circuit in Qiskit, adding extra quantum registers to some fixed list\n\n$\\newcommand{\\ket}[1]{|{#1}\\rangle}$ I have a Qiskit quantum circuit for multiplication $M$, that I want to onto another qiskit quantum circuit for inner product of vectors $I$. $M$ uses some ancillary qubits to perform the map $\\ket{a}\\ket{b}\\ket{c} \\mapsto \\ket{a}\\ket{b}\\ket{c\\oplus (a\\cdot b)}$. Ideally when I compose $M$ onto $I$ as a subroutine, I would only want to declare and map the quantum states (registers) of $I$ that I care about: $\\ket{a},\\ket{b},\\ket{c}$ and let the additional qubits be counted as some ancillas in $I$, maybe in some fixed register , whose length can be appropriately extended. According to the Qiskit documentation, to , $M$ can be narrower or of equal width to $I$, and methods like have been deprecated and removed. How can I go about doing this, if at all? I would be okay using any other SDK like Cirq, as I need a QASM2 at the end. But my circuits use classically controlled operations, which Cirq does not seem to support.\n\n```\ncompose\n```\n\n```\nanc\n```\n\n```\ncompose\n```\n\n```\nextend\n```\n\n```\ndump\n```\n\nOnly inspect and edit this task workspace and installed dependency APIs. Reference fixes, evaluation tests, other cases and previous repairs are unavailable inputs."
